hello and welcome to another Bitcoin  protocol tutorial video in this video  we're gonna be discussing the combining  and splitting values section of the  paper  this section is mostly just here to  clear up what some people might be  worrying about and that's how do we  handle varying amounts of Bitcoin that  are transacted up to now it's really  only been discussed in the idea of like  a full coin each time and obviously we  can split up a Bitcoin and trade  multiple at a time and that's part of  what makes it so great is that it's very  well divisible so hitting the paper  although would be possible to handle  coins individually it would be unwieldly  to make a separate transaction for every  cent in a transfer to allow value to be  split and combined transactions contain  multiple inputs and outputs normally  there will either be a single input from  a larger previous transaction or  multiple inputs combining smaller  amounts and at most two outputs one for  the payment and one returning the change  if any back to the sender it should be  noted that fan-out where a transaction  depends on several transactions and  those transactions depend on many more  is not a problem here there is never a  need to extract a complete standalone  copy of a transactions history so this  section is basically here to just  quickly dispel any fears about how the  system might be worthless since you can  easily since you wouldn't be able to  easily combine or divide a payment the  idea here is that you can have multiple  inputs for a transaction and you're  usually having two Max outputs the idea  here is that everything is quickly  cleaned up in a nice fancy way so to  track a payment you you wouldn't have to  figure out where that money fully came  from since you could consider this like  in exchange where when you buy one  Bitcoin you might get point two from a  person point five from another and point  three from another after that you might  only sell point seven etc so naturally  as time went on and that you know let's  say you sold point seven to a guy who's  buying a full one Bitcoin as well he  gets point seven from you point two from  another guy and point one from another  guy right  so obviously that would get really  really messy really quick and you can  see how it might be required that you  have a  full you know transaction history in  order to keep up with this stuff  so anyway not the case but yeah so not  not too much here just basically  dispelling that so anyway we're gonna  move on to the next section and that's  privacy as always thanks for watching
 

